meta {
  name: Create Lead
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "primaryContact": {
      "firstName": "Dana",
      "lastName": "Whitfield",
      "dateOfBirth": "1988-04-12",
      "phone": "(555) 222-3333",
      "email": "Dana.Whitfield@Example.com"
    },
    "address": {
      "street": "77 Oak Lane",
      "city": "Tulsa",
      "state": "OK",
      "zip": "74101"
    },
    "members": [
      {
        "firstName": "Sam",
        "lastName": "Whitfield",
        "dateOfBirth": "1990-06-01",
        "role": "Spouse"
      },
      {
        "firstName": "Kit",
        "lastName": "Whitfield",
        "role": "Child"
      }
    ],
    "policiesOfInterest": [
      { "policyType": "Auto", "itemCount": 2 },
      { "policyType": "Home", "itemCount": 1, "sameAsHousehold": true },
      {
        "policyType": "Landlord",
        "itemCount": 1,
        "sameAsHousehold": false,
        "propertyAddress": {
          "street": "4 Rental Row",
          "city": "Bixby",
          "state": "OK",
          "zip": "74008"
        }
      }
    ],
    "leadSourceCode": "WCO7l",
    "submissionToken": "{{$guid}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  // Capture for the Replay request, which must return this same id.
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setVar("createdLeadId", res.getBody().id);
    bru.setVar("createdLeadToken", req.getBody().submissionToken);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("returns only the new lead id", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("id");
    expect(Object.keys(body)).to.have.lengthOf(1);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /leads — create a lead from the New Lead form (PAC-37)

  Runs the full intake pipeline: resolve contact → derive household → resolve or
  create lead → link entities → assign producer. A port of legacy
  `processNewLead`, minus round-robin (see PAC-53).

  - **Module:** `leads`
  - **Permission:** `leads:write`
  - **Data scope:** `own` — the created lead is assigned to **the caller**.

  > `leads:write` is also held by **Agency Owner** and **Branch Manager**, so an
  > owner or manager who submits this form becomes the lead's producer. That
  > matches legacy (which stamped the current user with no role check) and is an
  > accepted trade-off, revisited in PAC-53.

  ## Body
  | field                   | type   | required | rules                                        |
  |-------------------------|--------|----------|----------------------------------------------|
  | primaryContact.firstName| string | yes      | 1–60 chars                                   |
  | primaryContact.lastName | string | yes      | 1–60 chars                                   |
  | primaryContact.dateOfBirth | string | yes   | `YYYY-MM-DD`                                 |
  | primaryContact.phone    | string | yes      | 10–20 chars; punctuation is stripped         |
  | primaryContact.email    | string | yes      | valid email; lowercased on write             |
  | address                 | object | no       | street / city / state / zip, the **living** address |
  | members[]               | array  | no       | max 10; role ∈ Spouse, Child, Driver, Additional Named Insured |
  | policiesOfInterest[]    | array  | no       | max 12; canonical labels, count 1–99; defaults to `[]` — see the row shape below |
  | policiesOfInterest[].policyType | string | yes | canonical label; a raw SmartSuite code is a `400` |
  | policiesOfInterest[].itemCount  | number | yes | integer 1–99                          |
  | policiesOfInterest[].sameAsHousehold | bool | no | defaults `true`; property rows only. When set, `address` is copied onto **this row** |
  | policiesOfInterest[].propertyAddress | object | cond. | required when the row is a property type **and** `sameAsHousehold` is false |
  | leadSourceCode          | string | yes      | canonical code; `ENEJP` (Test) is rejected   |
  | householdId             | string | no       | ObjectId (24 hex); pins the lead to an existing household — see below |
  | quoteControlNumber      | string | no       | dedupe signal 2                              |
  | submissionToken         | string | no       | 8–200 chars; per-form-session idempotency key |

  ## Behaviour worth knowing
  **Contact matching.** Candidates are found by exact (case-insensitive) first +
  last name, then scored: matching DOB +4, matching email +2, matching phone +2.
  A **conflicting** DOB disqualifies a candidate outright — two people sharing a
  name but not a birth date are different people. No positive score → a new
  contact is created. Creating a duplicate is recoverable; merging two people is
  not.

  **Household.** Derived from the resolved contact, never looked up by address —
  that is what stops an existing client acquiring a second household. Migrated
  contacts carrying only `legacyHouseholdId` are self-healed on first touch.

  **Pinning a household (`householdId`).** The Household page's "Start Quote"
  dialog creates the lead from a household the producer already has open, so
  the household is a fact rather than something to infer. Sending it inverts the
  usual step order — the household resolves *first* — and changes three things,
  all for the one reason that nothing may be inferred which could contradict it:

  1. The household is taken verbatim, scoped to the caller's agency. A household
     that does not exist there is a `404`, never a quiet fall back to deriving
     one; "create this lead on household X" landing somewhere else is the one
     outcome nobody could detect afterwards.
  2. **Contact matching is confined to that household's contacts.** An
     agency-wide name hit would be wrong twice over: linking moves the matched
     contact into this household, and the household they came from silently
     loses them. Confining it can at worst create a duplicate contact — the
     recoverable half of the trade this endpoint makes everywhere.
  3. **Address dedupe (signal 3) is confined to that household**, so a lead at
     the same street and zip belonging to *different* people — the apartment
     building and house-share case address matching is documented as unable to
     tell apart — is not returned as this one.

  It is accepted on this route only. `POST /public/leads/:token` uses the bare
  base schema, so an outside submitter can never name the household their
  submission lands in.

  **Lead dedupe**, in strict order: (1) `submissionToken` — an exact hit returns
  the existing lead untouched; (2) `quoteControlNumber`; (3) `addressKey`
  (`street|zip`, both lowercased) restricted to non-terminal leads created in the
  last 90 days (and to `householdId` when pinned). Signals 2 and 3 merge
  additively and never change `producerId`, `status` or `temperature`.

  > Worth knowing on the pinned path: because the dialog prefills the form from
  > the household, a second "new" lead for a household that already has an open
  > one at the same address dedupes onto it and returns its id. That is the
  > intended answer — the dialog lists those leads first — not a failure.

  **Policies of interest (PAC-56 #2).** What the submitter wants quoted — the
  Quote Recap's policy rows **minus premium**, since no quote exists yet. Same
  vocabulary (`Auto`, `Home`, `Renters`, `Condominium`, `Landlord`,
  `Motorcycle`, `Boat Owners`, `Umbrella`, `Life`, `Valuable Item Protection`,
  `Auto - Special`); an unrecognised value is a `400`, it is a fixed vocabulary
  and not free text. Optional here while the web form requires at least one,
  exactly as `address` is: the endpoint would rather store an incomplete lead
  than reject one. On a dedupe merge (signals 2 and 3) the rows are **unioned by
  policy type *and* dwelling** into the existing lead — a restated type at the
  same address keeps the count already on file, while the same type at a
  different address is a second row (a landlord adding a second rental is
  stating a second interest, not restating the first). Legacy captured no
  equivalent at intake; every migrated lead has `[]`.

  > **Which form sends this.** The **public** intake form (`POST
  > /public/leads/:token`) collects it — PAC-56 #2 scopes the question to the
  > prospect. The internal `/leads/new` form does **not**, and omits the field
  > entirely; a producer records what was quoted on the Quote Recap instead, with
  > real premiums. The endpoint accepts it on both routes because the schema is
  > shared and PAC-53's white-label forms will post here too — this request
  > exercises that path.

  **Property address (PAC-56 #6/#14).** Lives on the **policy row**, not on the
  submission: a prospect can ask about the home they live in *and* a rental they
  let out, and one lead-level address describes only one of them. Asked only for
  a property-type row (`Home`, `Renters`, `Condominium`, `Landlord`) and stored
  **already resolved** — with `sameAsHousehold: true` the server copies `address`
  onto that row and **discards** any `propertyAddress` sent with it, so a row
  cannot claim one thing and store another. With it false, a blank address is a
  `400`. **Nothing is stored on a non-property row**, which is what stops the
  `sameAsHousehold: true` default from quietly stamping an address onto every
  Auto-only lead.

  > The **lead-level** `propertyAddress` is no longer written. It stays on the
  > document for migrated records (SmartSuite `Property Address`, `sfd5ba053e`)
  > and for leads captured before #14, and `GET /leads/:id` still returns it —
  > read `policiesOfInterest[].propertyAddress` first and fall back to it.

  **Atomicity.** Lead, household and contacts are written in one MongoDB
  transaction; a failure part-way leaves no partial records.

  **Idempotency.** Send the same `submissionToken` twice — from a double-click or
  a retry — and you get the same lead id, with no second record. Even two
  genuinely concurrent requests resolve to one lead.

  ## Response `201`
  ```json
  { "id": "665f0a1b2c3d4e5f60718293" }
  ```
  Deliberately just the id — the client navigates to `/leads/:id`.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — validation failure; body is `{ message, errors }` with
    zod's flattened field errors.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `leads` module disabled, missing `leads:write`, a
    platform-admin caller with no agency, or an agency with no default branch.
  - `404 Not Found` — `householdId` names no household in the caller's agency.
}
